# No trailing slash on purpose. A pattern ending in `/` matches directories only,
# and git treats a SYMLINK as a file — so `node_modules/` silently fails to ignore
# a `node_modules` symlink pointing at another checkout, and `git add -A` commits
# the link, publishing that checkout's path. Verified, not assumed.
node_modules
dist/
.DS_Store
*.log
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.tsbuildinfo

# --- Generated by scripts/install.sh -----------------------------------------
# These are the ACTIVE config files. They are produced from the *.default.json
# templates plus config/providers/*.json plus your answers, and they carry your
# endpoints — a workspace host, a tenant name, a local port. The install layout
# symlinks ~/.pi/agent/* at this directory, so they have to live here rather than
# outside the repo; ignoring them is what stops a fork from ever publishing them.
# Track config/*.default.json instead, and re-run the installer to regenerate.
#
# This list must stay equal to $GENERATED_CONFIGS in scripts/install.sh plus the
# three it handles separately (models, routing, mcp). It cannot be a wildcard:
# config/ also holds a dozen tracked files that the installer never writes, and
# `config/*.json` would untrack all of them. bin/pi-check enforces the equality
# so the two lists cannot drift apart silently.
config/models.json
config/routing.json
config/mcp.json
config/settings.json
config/guard.json
config/trusted-roots.json
config/path-defaults.json
config/web.json
config/web-search.json
config/quota.json
config/subagent.json

# --- Identity -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The operator-identity extension refuses to READ a personal identity file from a
# path inside this repository; these patterns make sure one dropped here by hand
# can never be COMMITTED either. `bin/pi-check` rule PC-12 fails the build if such
# a file ever becomes trackable.
OPERATOR.local.md
PRIVATE.md

# --- Your skills and sub-agents -----------------------------------------------
# This repo ships the skill and sub-agent *loading mechanisms* but no content.
# Anything you write goes in these two directories; neither is ever part of a
# commit.
#
# `skills/` used to be three directories — `skills/`, `skills-work/` and
# `skills-private/`. That split read as a privacy boundary and was not one: only
# `skills-private/` was ignored here, and the other two were visible to PI only
# because an extension contributed them at runtime, which is why neither ever
# appeared in config/settings.json's `skills` array. One root, ignored here and
# declared once in settings, is the honest shape. `bin/pi-check` rule PC-12 fails
# the repository if anything under it becomes trackable.
#
# Both patterns are ROOT-ANCHORED. Unanchored, `skills/` would also swallow
# `examples/skills/`, which is tracked on purpose: it holds the one worked
# example, deliberately outside every search path so PI never loads it.
/skills/
/agents-private/

# --- Sub-agent run state -------------------------------------------------------
# Per-run child state: transcripts, `_input.md`/`_output.md`, `_meta.json` cost
# records and `missions/*.json`, written into the cwd of whatever session
# dispatched a sub-agent. Both noise and a leak surface — it holds task text and
# model spend.
.pi-subagents/

# --- Leak-check patterns (PC-23) ------------------------------------------------
# The optional local source of PC-23's forbidden-pattern list — an internal hostname,
# an employer name, whatever a private-to-public port must never carry across. One
# pattern per line. Ignored here so the file that names the secrets cannot itself
# become the leak; PI_LEAK_CHECK_PATTERNS (an env var) is the other, equally valid
# source. See bin/rules/pc-23-no-configured-leak-patterns.mjs.
config/leak-patterns.local.txt

# --- Documentation build -------------------------------------------------------
site/
.cache/
